{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And all his daughters</b> — his daughter and his son's daughter. It is also plausible that his daughters-in-law are included in this category, for daughters-in-law too are subsumed under the term \"daughters.\" Or as the Sages say (Bereshit Rabbah 84:19), \"A person does not refrain from calling his daughters-in-law 'daughters.'\" So too Naomi said to her daughters-in-law, \"Go\" (Ruth 1:8), \"Return, my daughters\" (Ruth 1:12), \"No, my daughters\" (Ruth 1:13), \"Go, my daughter\" (Ruth 2:2) — and this is nothing but a way of affection alone, like \"Have you not heard, my daughter?\" (Ruth 2:8)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the midrash by chapter and paragraph; I converted to standard chapter:verse for the biblical references, retaining the Bereshit Rabbah citation as he gives it (פ\"פ י\"ט → 84:19)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:22.607384290Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}